    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/open-science-advisor-08-10-fte-0": {
      "title": "Open Science Advisor (0.8",
      "description": "Open Science Advisor (0.8 - 1.0 FTE) | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Featured results Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Open Science Advisor (0.8 - 1.0 FTE) Listen Published Wednesday 14 Oct 2026 Deadline Wednesday 28 Oct 2026 Work area Policy & Advice Organisational unit Engagement & Research Services (ERS) Salary € 4.925 - € 6.682 Employment 0,8 fte - 1 fte Introduction Are you a passionate advocate for open science and open scholarship? Do you have first-hand experience implementing open scholarship practices in the research workflow? Then don’t hesitate to read on and apply! Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) is hiring an Open Science Advisor to increase the awareness and adoption of open scholarship practices among researchers and professional services staff. You will join Engagement & Research Services (ERS) -- a dynamic department within Professional Services at EUR -- and join the Open & Responsible Science (ORS) team of community and engagement managers, data stewards, and ethics professionals, to achieve the ambitious goal of making open scholarship the default way of working at EUR. Job description Your main tasks and responsibilities will be the following: Advice for Research Inform and advise academics, professional services, and management on how open scholarship practices can increase academic and societal impact. Example: you are invited to provide feedback on the open science policy of a faculty. Inform and advise academics in creating, describing, publishing, using, and archiving results of academic research and impact activities in line with open scholarship principles. Example: you provide a researcher with the necessary information for them to start their first preregistration or to apply open scholarship principles to their engaged research project. Advise and support academics and grant officers on how to organically embed open scholarship in funding applications. Example: you discuss with an NWO Vidi grant applicant and a grant officer what (project-specific) information on open scholarship could be included in the funding application. Teaching Contribute to improving education and training on open scholarship by developing, maintaining, and/or teaching courses and workshops. Example: you update the current EUR ORS MOOC in close collaboration with a project coordinator, the coordinator of the educational ORS framework, and other experts in- and outside EUR. Collaboration You cooperate with other units at ERS (Research Management, Grant Support, and Innovation & Partnerships), departments at EUR (e.g., library, IT), the Hub for Impactful & Engaged Research, and strategy programs (e.g., Recognition & Rewards) to increase awareness, facilitate support, and align institutional activities (directly and indirectly) related to open scholarship. Example: you help organize an event on how open scholarship can support the diversification of academic career paths, e.g., by brainstorming specific topics of interest and inviting internal/external speakers from relevant networks. You advise on and co-formulate the ORS ambitions for the EUR, in close collaborations with academic and professional services staff as well as School and executive management. Other You may dedicate some time to research, to keep up to date and contribute to (inter)national conversations on open scholarship. Example: you co-author an opinion piece on new ways to involve citizens in co-creating research questions that are relevant for academics and the public alike. Job requirements You recognize yourself in the following description: You have an academic background (minimum MSc/MA) You adhere to the values of open scholarship and support their practical implementation You have first-hand experience with various open scholarship practices (e.g., open data, open code, societal engagement) You effectively and persuasively communicate with people coming from different (academic) SSH disciplines and in various stages in their career, from a PhD candidate to a Dean Your networking skills allow you to build bridges and mitigate conflict in order to achieve a common goal You are meticulous (e.g., in advising on, writing, and reviewing policy) You are fluent in English (minimum CEFR-level C1) Recommended (experience in the following would be a bonus): You have completed a PhD in a discipline where open scholarship is actively discussed (e.g., psychology) You have experience in teaching open scholarship values, practices, and/or tools (e.g., by means of lectures or workshops) You have (co-)authored documents (e.g., blog posts, opinion pieces, academic papers, policy documents) discussing open scholarship You read Dutch (minimum B1) Employment conditions and benefits We offer you an internationally oriented and varied job in an enthusiastic team, with excellent working conditions in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO-NU). The start date of this position is 1 January 2027 and you will be based at campus Woudestein in Engagement & Research Services (ERS). This position is for 0.8 fte - 1 fte. The salary is dependent on your experience and knowledge and ranges from a minimum of € 4.925 to a maximum of € 6.682 gross per month Scale 11 on a fulltime basis (38 hours), in accordance with the CAO-NU. The contract is offered for a period of 1 year and can be converted into an employment contract for an indefinite period if suitability is proven and if continued in the same position, if (financial and/or organizational) circumstances remain the same. Everything else we offer you; you can find below! - Everything you need for a good work-life balance : the option to work from home in consultation with your manager, 41 days of paid leave with a 40 hour contract, 8% holiday pay and an 8.3% end-of-year bonus, and a significant discount on a subscription for our on-campus sports centre Opens external ! - Sustainable, inclusive and diverse work environment with an open culture, where you can be yourself and we pay attention to each other and to the world around us. Make the most of our bicycle budget, or join networks such as Young@EUR Opens external , FAME , EUR Disability Staff Network or QuEUR. - Time and space for your development in the broadest sense: development days and a personal career budget and free access to our university library . - Good pension with ABP that EUR contributes 2/3rds towards, discounts on various collective insurances with Zilveren Kruis Achmea, Loyalis, and Allianz, and compensation for travel, working from home and home internet use. - Are you currently combining your job with parenthood, or do you want to do so in the future? EUR offers partially paid parental leave and fully paid additional birth leave for partners , and our campus features a daycare (daycare not paid for by EUR). - Moving to the Netherlands for your job with EUR? Then you may be eligible for the Expat-ruling if you meet the requirements of the Belastingdienst (Dutch tax agency), and with our Dual Career Programme we will also help your partner find the right job for them. Employer Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) is an internationally oriented university with a strong social orientation in its education and research, as expressed in our mission ‘Creating positive societal impact’. EUR is home to 4.100 academics and professionals and almost 33.000 students from more than 140 countries. Everything we do, we do under the credo The Erasmian Way – Making Minds Matter. We’re global citizens, connecting, entrepreneurial, open-minded, and socially involved. These Erasmian Values function as our internal compass and create EUR’s distinctive and recognizable profile. From these values, with a broad perspective and with an eye for diversity, different backgrounds and opinions, our employees work closely together to solve societal challenges from the dynamic and cosmopolitan city of Rotterdam. Thanks to the high quality and positive societal impact of our research and education, EUR can compete with the top European universities. www.eur.nl . Faculty / Institute / Central service Engagement & Research Services (ERS) advances excellent research and engagement to maximize positive societal impact. ERS creates conditions for transparent, inclusive, collaborative, and impactful research through strategic and operational expertise. We provide pathways for talents to reach their full potential and we advance fruitful collaborations, enabling Erasmus University to be a front runner in embedding excellent research in society. The following areas of support are embedded within ERS: Business Development, Grant Support, Legal Counsels, Open Science, Research Data Management, Ethics Review and Research Intelligence. ERS is a relatively young service department within Erasmus University Rotterdam and is steadily building and extending its service portfolio. ERS provides support to all faculties of Erasmus University Rotterdam (occasionally to Erasmus University Medical Center). Department Within ERS, the Open & Responsible Science team provides professional advice and support to enable the adoption of ORS principles and innovative research practices within EUR. Additional information For additional information, please contact Office Engagement and Research Services ( office.ers@eur.nl ). Applying Please submit your application, consisting of a one-page CV and a one-page motivation letter, no later than October 28th, 2026, before 23:59 CET . The selection procedure consists of two rounds of interviews. The first round will take place on November 10th 2026. The second round will be held on November 16th 2026 . In case of equal suitability, internal candidates will be preferred. We appreciate a disclosure if LLMs are used to generate the CV and/or the cover letter. Recruitment and selection agencies or headhunters: thank you for thinking along with us, but we prefer to recruit our candidates ourselves. We look forward to receiving your application! Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Job alert Please select your preferences. When a new job becomes available that matches these preference we'll send you an email. Fields marked with a * are required. First Name Last Name Email address Password Your password requires a minimum of 8 characters of which 2 non-alphabetic Confirm password Business Unit Any Erasmus School of Economics (ESE) Erasmus School of Law (ESL) Erasmus School of History, Culture and Communication (ESHCC) Erasmus School of Social and Behavioural Sciences (ESSB) Erasmus School of Philosophy (ESPhil) Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) International Institute of Social Studies (ISS) Erasmus School of Health Policy & Management (ESHPM) General Management Directorate (ABD) University Library (UB) Convergence Human Resources (HR) Finance (FIN) Education and Student Affairs (E&S) Erasmus Digitalisation and Information Services (EDIS) Real Estate & Facilities (RE&F) Engagement & Research Services (ERS) Marketing & Communications (M&C) RSM BV Institute for Housing and Urban Development Studies (IHS) Any Any Erasmus School of Economics (ESE) Erasmus School of Law (ESL) Erasmus School of History, Culture and Communication (ESHCC) Erasmus School of Social and Behavioural Sciences (ESSB) Erasmus School of Philosophy (ESPhil) Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) International Institute of Social Studies (ISS) Erasmus School of Health Policy & Management (ESHPM) General Management Directorate (ABD) University Library (UB) Convergence Human Resources (HR) Finance (FIN) Education and Student Affairs (E&S) Erasmus Digitalisation and Information Services (EDIS) Real Estate & Facilities (RE&F) Engagement & Research Services (ERS) Marketing & Communications (M&C) RSM BV Institute for Housing and Urban Development Studies (IHS) OK Cancel Expertise Any Administration & Secretariat Policy & Advice IT Marketing & Communication Human Resources Finance Legal Real Estate & Facilities Management Education Research, development, innovation Academic staff PhD Procurement Any Any Administration & Secretariat Policy & Advice IT Marketing & Communication Human Resources Finance Legal Real Estate & Facilities Management Education Research, development, innovation Academic staff PhD Procurement OK Cancel Share this page Interested? Apply now Applicant portal Open navigation for Open Science Advisor (0.8 - 1.0 FTE) Close Interested? Apply now Interested? Apply now Compare 0 study programmes Close Compare 0 study programmes 0 of 3 study programmes selected. @title Duration: @duration Remove @title Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies Open Science Advisor (0.8 - 1.0 FTE) Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close Our website uses cookies Overview This website uses cookies and similar technologies (hereinafter collectively: 'cookies'). In this menu, you can choose which types of cookies and purposes you consent to. Functional: ​​These cookies are technically necessary for the website to function properly. Using the website is not possible without accepting these cookies. Analytics: These cookies are used to analyze and improve website usage in general. We take measures to ensure that your privacy is not, or only minimally, affected by these cookies. It's highly appreciated if you toggle the switch. Marketing & Social Media: If you want marketing campaigns and advertisements you see online and on social media to be tailored to your browsing behavior and the interests derived from it, you can enable the slider or click the 'Accept all' button. You can change or withdraw your consent at any time by making new choices in the menu. Click on 'Overview' at the top for a complete overview of the cookies in each category. Please see our cookies page for more information and to reset your cookie preferences. Functional Analytics Marketing & Social Media Reject all Accept Selection Accept all Eur.nl Functional Cookies and similar technologies that are strictly necessary for the website to work, such as remembering your preferences, security, enabling log-in or processing forms. Consent Studio Consent Studio consent-studio__consent-id ID of the proof of consent storage within the Consent Studio platform. 30 days consent-studio__storage Local storage for the consent given by the user for certain categories of cookies. 30 days consent-studio__seen Boolean that keeps track of whether the user has seen the cookie banner at least once. 30 days consent-studio__implicit-consent-triggered When a website has implicit consent configured, this cookie stores whether or not it has been triggered or not. 30 days consent-studio__gpc-consent-triggered Records that