    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/research-ethics-facilitator-08-10-fte": {
      "title": "Research Ethics Facilitator (0.8 - 1.0 FTE)",
      "description": "Research Ethics Facilitator (0.8 - 1.0 FTE) | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Research Ethics Facilitator (0.8 - 1.0 FTE) Listen Published Wednesday 4 Nov 2026 Deadline Friday 20 Nov 2026 Work area Administration & secretariat Organisational unit Engagement & Research Services (ERS) Salary € 3.873 - € 5.264 Employment 0,8 fte - 1 fte Introduction Would you like to enable bold and impactful research by supporting researchers in the fields of social sciences and humanities? Do you believe in the importance of ethical, socially engaged, and responsible research practices? Then we look forward to meeting you! Engagement & Research Services (ERS) is looking for a Research Ethics Facilitator to provide direct support to faculty Research Ethics Review Committees (RERCs) in the humanities and social sciences. As part of the ERS Open & Responsible Science (ORS) team, you will work together with the university’s community and engagement managers, data stewards, and other ethics professionals to establish open and responsible scholarship as the default way of working at EUR. You will perform secretarial, administrative, and organisation-related work for the RERCs. In this role, you will be supporting the RERCs at The International Institute of Social Studies (ISS), Erasmus School of Law (ESL), Erasmus School of History, Culture and Communication (ESHCC), and the Institute for Housing and Urban Development Studies (IHS). The RERCs evaluate applications for ethics review submitted by researchers in the Schools and Institutes. You will ensure that the RERCs are provided with optimum support in decision-making and in the implementation of the EUR ethics review policy. Additionally, you will work together with the central Research Ethics team to raise awareness and provide both guidance and resources on the research ethics review process at EUR. Job description Your main tasks and responsibilities will be: You are the key person in the ethics review process: you screen incoming applications, assign them to reviewers, and communicate with researchers on behalf of the RERCs You are the first point of contact for researchers, Committee Chairs, and members of the RERCs, the Coordinator Research Ethics, and the policy officers of the Schools regarding the ethics review content and process You advise researchers in preparation for and during the ethics review process You organise and support RERC meetings You collaborate with the other Research Ethics team members in EUR-wide projects, including organising events, and developing training materials, guidelines, best practices You participate in (inter)national professional networks You will work from the offices of ERS and the Schools that you support, at Campus Woudestein (Rotterdam) and The International Institute of Social Studies (The Hague), on site for a minimum of 3 days a week (2 at EUR's Woudestein campus and 1 in The Hague). Job requirements You recognise yourself in the following description: You have an academic background (master’s degree or PhD) You act with integrity and critically examine, reflect, and solve problems in a proactive and collaborative way You are organised and work accurately with an attention to detail You effectively communicate with, and mediate between, people coming from different (academic) backgrounds You feel confident leading and organising workshops, information sessions, and trainings You are able to work independently as well as in a team You are fluent in English (minimum CEFR-level C1) You read and understand Dutch (minimum B2) Desirable: You have an academic background in social sciences, behavioural sciences, or humanities You have experience supporting, or serving on, a committee You have experience with administrative work You have knowledge of research ethics and/or ethics review processes You have experience with initiating, developing, implementing, and seeing to completion guidelines and projects You have basic knowledge of the GDPR, including knowledge of informed consent You write and speak fluent Dutch (C1 and above) Employment conditions and benefits We offer you an internationally oriented and varied job in an enthusiastic team, with excellent working conditions in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO-NU). The start date of this position is 1 February 2027 and you will be based at Campus Woudestein in Engagement & Research Services (ERS). This position is for 0.8 fte - 1 fte. The salary is dependent on your experience and knowledge and ranges from a minimum of € 3.873 to a maximum of € 5.264 gross per month Scale 9 on a fulltime basis (38 hours), in accordance with the CAO-NU. The contract is offered for a period of 1 year and can be converted into an employment contract for an indefinite period if suitability is proven and if continued in the same position, if (financial and/or organizational) circumstances remain the same. Everything else we offer you; you can find below! - Everything you need for a good work-life balance : the option to work from home in consultation with your manager, 41 days of paid leave with a 40 hour contract, 8% holiday pay and an 8.3% end-of-year bonus, and a significant discount on a subscription for our on-campus sports centre ! - Sustainable, inclusive and diverse work environment with an open culture, where you can be yourself and we pay attention to each other and to the world around us. Make the most of our bicycle budget, or join networks such as Young@EUR , FAME , EUR Disability Staff Network or QuEUR. - Time and space for your development in the broadest sense: development days and a personal career budget , international exchange opportunities, with most expenses covered by us. and free access to our university library . - Good pension with ABP that EUR contributes 2/3rds towards, discounts on various collective insurances with Zilveren Kruis Achmea, Loyalis, and Allianz, and compensation for travel, working from home and home internet use. - Are you currently combining your job with parenthood, or do you want to do so in the future? EUR offers partially paid parental leave and fully paid additional birth leave for partners , and our campus features a daycare (daycare not paid for by EUR). - Moving to the Netherlands for your job with EUR? Then you may be eligible for the Expat-ruling if you meet the requirements of the Belastingdienst (Dutch tax agency), and with our Dual Career Programme we will also help your partner find the right job for them. Employer Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) is an internationally oriented university with a strong social orientation in its education and research, as expressed in our mission ‘Creating positive societal impact’. EUR is home to 4.100 academics and professionals and almost 33.000 students from more than 140 countries. Everything we do, we do under the credo The Erasmian Way – Making Minds Matter. We’re global citizens, connecting, entrepreneurial, open-minded, and socially involved. These Erasmian Values function as our internal compass and create EUR’s distinctive and recognizable profile. From these values, with a broad perspective and with an eye for diversity, different backgrounds and opinions, our employees work closely together to solve societal challenges from the dynamic and cosmopolitan city of Rotterdam. Thanks to the high quality and positive societal impact of our research and education, EUR can compete with the top European universities. www.eur.nl . Faculty / Institute / Central service Engagement & Research Services (ERS) advances excellent research and engagement to maximize positive societal impact. ERS creates conditions for transparent, inclusive, collaborative, and impactful research through strategic and operational expertise. We provide pathways for talents to reach their full potential and we advance fruitful collaborations, enabling Erasmus University to be a front runner in embedding excellent research in society. The following areas of support are embedded within ERS: Business Development, Grant Support, Legal Counsels, Open Science, Research Data Management, Ethics Review and Research Intelligence. ERS is a relatively young service department within Erasmus University Rotterdam and is steadily building and extending its service portfolio. ERS provides support to all faculties of Erasmus university Rotterdam (occasionally to Erasmus University Medical Center). Department Within ERS, the Open & Responsible Science team provides professional advice and support to enable the adoption of ORS principles and innovative research practices within EUR. Additional information For additional information, please contact Office Engagement & Research Services ( office.ers@eur.nl ). Applying Please submit your application, consisting of a one-page CV and a one-page motivation letter, no later than Friday, November 20, 2026, before 23:59 CET . The selection procedure consists of two rounds of interviews. The first round will take place on Thursday, December 3rd, 2026 . The second round will be held on Tuesday, December 8th, 2026 . In case of equal suitability, internal candidates will be preferred. We appreciate a disclosure if LLMs are used to generate the CV and/or the cover letter. Recruitment and selection agencies or headhunters: thank you for thinking along with us, but we prefer to recruit our candidates ourselves. We look forward to receiving your application! Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for Research Ethics Facilitator (0.8 - 1.0 FTE) Close Interested? Interested? Compare 0 study programme Close Compare 0 study programme Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies Research Ethics Facilitator (0.8 - 1.0 FTE) Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/tenure-track-assistant-professor-applied-economics-10-fte": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Applied Economics (1.0 fte)",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Applied Economics (1.0 fte) | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Applied Economics (1.0 fte) Listen Published Tuesday 3 Nov 2026 Deadline Tuesday 1 Dec 2026 Work area Academic staff Organisational unit Erasmus School of Economics (ESE) Salary € 4.925 - € 6.682 Employment 1 fte - 1 fte Introduction The Department of Applied Economics at Erasmus School of Economics (ESE), Erasmus University Rotterdam, the Netherlands, invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position, starting on 1 September 2027. The position is open to candidates with a strong research profile in any of our three fields: behavioural economics, health economics or strategy economics. Our goal: Strengthen our research and teaching in behavioural, health and strategy economics, with research that helps firms, consumers and governments make better decisions. Your colleagues: You will join one of our three sections, in a department with a vibrant research environment. Job description As Assistant Professor, you will join one of our three sections and do research and teach in that section. You are expected to publish in leading journals and engage in internal and external collaborations. You will teach in our bachelor and master programmes, and supervise bachelor and master theses. We also expect you to apply for external research funding, for example from NWO or the European Research Council (ERC). The department supports you in this. Job requirements What you bring Academic background – You hold a PhD in economics. Candidates who are close to completing their PhD, or who already hold a tenure-track position, are also invited to apply. Strong research profile – Your research fits one or more of our sections, and you show the potential to publish in leading journals. Teaching ability – You are a motivated teacher, ideally with teaching experience at university level, for example as lecturer or teaching assistant. Funding potential – You are willing to apply for external research funding. Collaborative mindset – You value teamwork and collegiality, and you want to contribute to your section and the department. Willingness to learn or ability to speak Dutch is considered a plus Our main selection criteria are outstanding research potential and teaching ability. Employment conditions and benefits We offer you an internationally oriented and varied job in an enthusiastic team, with excellent working conditions in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO-NU). The starting date of this position is 1 September 2027 and you will be based at Campus Woudestein, Burgemeester Oudlaan 50 in Rotterdam. This position is open for 1.0 fte. The salary is dependent on your experience and knowledge and ranges from a minimum of € 4.925,- to a maximum of € 6.682, - gross per month, scale 11 on a fulltime basis (38 hours), in accordance with the CAO-NU. Your position consists of a temporary employment contract as Assistant Professor on a tenure track for a duration of 6 years. At the start of the fourth year a department committee will formally assess whether you are on track towards meeting the final tenure criteria. In case you are on track, you already will be granted a permanent contract as Assistant Professor. After 5.5 years, a formal evaluation takes place with respect to research performance, teaching performance and contribution to the Department. When this evaluation is successful, you will be promoted to Associate Professor. When this evaluation is unsuccessful, your employment contract will be terminated at the end of year 6. In case of special personal circumstances, for instance combining work and care, the timing of the formal evaluation can be postponed. Everything else we offer you, you can find below! Everything you need for a good work-life balance : the option to work from home in consultation with your manager, 41 days of paid leave with a 40 hour contract, 8% holiday pay and an 8.3% end-of-year bonus, and a significant discount on a subscription for our on-campus sports centre ! Sustainable, inclusive and diverse work environment with an open culture, where you can be yourself and we pay attention to each other and to the world around us. Make the most of our bicycle budget, or join networks such as Young@EUR , FAME or QuEUR. Time and space for your development in the broadest sense: development days and a personal career budget and free access to our university library . Good pension with ABP that EUR contributes 2/3rds towards, discounts on various collective insurances with Zilveren Kruis Achmea, Loyalis, and Allianz, and compensation for travel, working from home and home internet use. Are you currently combining your job with parenthood, or do you want to do so in the future? EUR offers partially paid parental leave and fully paid additional birth leave for partners , and our campus features a daycare (daycare not paid for by EUR). Moving to the Netherlands for your job with EUR? Then you may be eligible for the 30%-ruling if you meet the requirements of the Belastingdienst (Dutch tax agency), and with our Dual Career Programme we will also help your partner find the right job for them. Employer Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) is an internationally oriented university with a strong social orientation in its education and research, as expressed in our mission ‘Creating positive societal impact’. EUR is home to 4.100 academics and professionals and almost 33.000 students from more than 140 countries. Everything we do, we do under the credo The Erasmian Way – Making Minds Matter. We’re global citizens, connecting, entrepreneurial, open-minded, and socially involved. These Erasmian Values function as our internal compass and create EUR’s distinctive and recognizable profile. From these values, with a broad perspective and with an eye for diversity, different backgrounds and opinions, our employees work closely together to solve societal challenges from the dynamic and cosmopolitan city of Rotterdam. Thanks to the high quality and positive societal impact of our research and education, EUR can compete with the top European universities. www.eur.nl . Faculty / Institute / Central service Erasmus School of Economics (ESE) is a leading school in economics in the Netherlands. It has a strong international reputation and aims to do outstanding research. Moreover, it provides excellent education at the BA., MA. and Ph.D. levels. Much research at Erasmus School of Economics is policy relevant, has social impact, and offers a strong research culture, ample research resources, and comparatively low teaching loads. Department Our department The Department of Applied Economics works on economic questions that matter for society. We aim for world-leading research that firms, consumers and governments can use. Our researchers are affiliated with the Tinbergen Institute, one of Europe's leading research institutes and graduate schools in economics, econometrics and finance. They are also affiliated with the Erasmus Research Institute of Management (ERIM), where we work with colleagues from Rotterdam School of Management (RSM). The department has three sections: Behavioural Economics – We study how people make decisions under risk and uncertainty, over time, and in social settings. We want to understand what shapes behaviour, and we design interventions that help people make better decisions. Topics include health behaviour, financial decisions, security, and unethical behaviour such as lying and cheating. We combine decision theory with field, laboratory and online experiments. Health Economics – We are one of the larger groups of health economists at a school of economics in Europe. Our main topics are health inequality, health behaviour, global health, social science genetics, and various topics at the intersection of health and labour economics. Most of our work is empirical, often using quasi-experimental methods and linked administrative data on the Dutch population. Strategy Economics – We do theory-based empirical research on the organisation of firms, industries and markets. Our fields include applied industrial organisation, entrepreneurship, innovation, and regional and urban economics. The section sits at the intersection of economics and business studies, and targets top journals in both economics and management. Our staff also carry out studies for policy makers and industry. There are many links between the three sections. We particularly, but not exclusively, welcome candidates whose work connects two or more sections. Applying Interested? Please apply no later than 1  December 2026 Your application should include: Curriculum vitae (CV) Cover letter (max. 1 page). Please state which section(s) your work fits best: Behavioural Economics, Health Economics and/or Strategy Economics. Job market paper Research statement (max. 2 pages), outlining your research plans and how they fit the department Teaching evaluations (if available) Two reference letters We kindly ask you to apply via the website (not by e-mail!). Interviews will take place online in December 2026 and January 2027. For questions about the position, please contact the head of the section closest to your field at our central emailaddress: apec-hiring@ese.eur.nl Behavioural Economics: Georg Granic Health Economics: Hans van Kippersluis Strategy Economics: Jolanda Hessels More about the department: https://www.eur.nl/en/ese/department-applied-economics We look forward to getting to know you! Erasmus University Rotterdam continuously strives to be an equitable and inclusive employer and invites all applicants, regardless of background gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, religion, ethnicity, age, disability, or any other aspect that makes someone unique, to apply. Recruitment and selection agencies or headhunters: thank you for your interest, but we prefer to recruit our candidates ourselves. Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Applied Economics (1.0 fte) Close Interested? Interested? Compare 0 study programme Close Compare 0 study programme Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Applied Economics (1.0 fte) Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/interim-secretary-rsm-department-dean": {
      "title": "Interim Secretary RSM Department of the Dean",
      "description": "Interim Secretary RSM Department of the Dean | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Interim Secretary RSM Department of the Dean Listen Published Tuesday 3 Nov 2026 Deadline Friday 20 Nov 2026 Work area Administration & secretariat Organisational unit Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) Salary € 3.537 - € 4.673 Employment 0.6 fte - 0.8 fte Introduction At Rotterdam School of Management, Erasmus University (RSM) , we are looking for an interim secretary to work in the Department of the Dean. In this role at 0.8FTE, you are a temporary cover for an absent colleague. Your contract will be for an initial period of six (6) months and we can review it together after this period. Proactive and highly organised, you provide administrative and secretarial support to both the Dean's Office and the RSM Faculty Council . In this role, you report to the Dean's Office facilities and services manager and you work closely with a wide range of internal stakeholders from across the school. . Job description As RSM secretary in the Department of the Dean, you provide professional administrative and secretarial support to the Dean ‘s Office as well as to the RSM Faculty Council. Your Dean's Office responsibilities (approximately 0.6 FTE): Provide full secretarial support to the Dean's Office together with two other secretaries. Manage and respond to emails within shared departmental mailboxes to ensure timely follow-up and appropriate action. Coordinate meetings, appointments and complex agendas for stakeholders while taking priorities and preferences of the attendees into account. Draft, edit and format correspondence, letters, presentations, declarations and other documents. Arrange travel and logistics as required. Prepare and distribute meeting minutes, including meetings conducted in both Dutch and English. Act as a first point of contact for visitors and internal stakeholders. Support the organisation of business and social events. Provide back-up support to colleagues within the secretarial team. Perform ad-hoc administrative and operational support as required. Your Faculty Council responsibilities (approximately 0.2 FTE): Facilitate and support Faculty Council officers and members in carrying out their duties. Coordinate meeting schedules, room bookings and logistics. Prepare and distribute meeting agendas and supporting documentation. Draft and edit meeting minutes of Faculty Council meetings and, where required, subcommittee meetings. Distribute meeting minutes and documentation for review and approval. Organise annual Faculty Council training sessions. Edit and distribute as needed Faculty Council documentation. Format official letters and communications issued by the Faculty Council. Ensure agendas, meeting minutes and other official Faculty Council documents are published on the appropriate page on the RSM website. Provide additional administrative support related to the Faculty Council activities. Job requirements What we require from you: EUR/RSM employee or eligible to work within the university environment. Experience working in an international and multicultural environment. Experience working in a secretarial, administrative support, executive assistant, or similar role. Excellent written and verbal communication skills in English. Some command of Dutch is preferred. Strong proficiency in Microsoft 365 applications, particularly Outlook, Teams, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint. What competencies you offer us: As a successful candidate for this role you are: Service-oriented, professional, reliable and approachable. Highly organised and you pay excellent attention to detail. Able to prioritise effectively and manage competing deadlines. Comfortable working with multiple stakeholders and handling confidential information with discretion. A strong team player who contributes positively to shared workflows and collective responsibilities. Flexible and can adapt quickly to changing priorities. Skilled in minute-taking and in producing accurate written records. Proactive, solution-oriented and responsive. Capable of working independently while maintaining excellent communication with colleagues. Experienced in managing multiple tasks simultaneously while maintaining accuracy and quality. Employment conditions and benefits We offer you an internationally oriented and versatile job in an enthusiastic team, with excellent working conditions in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO-NU) . Your start date for this position is 1 December 2026 and you will be based at [ class.26087] at Rotterdam School of Management, Erasmus University (RSM) . This position is for 0.6 FTE - 0.8 FTE. The salary is dependent on your experience and knowledge and it ranges from a minimum of € 3,537 to a maximum of € 4,673 gross per month, Scale 8 based on fulltime employment (38 hours), in accordance with the CAO-NU. We can offer you this contract for an initial period of six (6) months. The initial contract can be converted into an employment contract for an indefinite period if suitability is proven and if continued in the same position, if (financial and/or organisational) circumstances remain the same. Everything else we offer you; you can find below! - Everything you need for a good work-life balance : the option to work from home in consultation with your manager, 41 days of paid leave with a 40 hour contract, 8% holiday pay and an 8.3% end-of-year bonus, and a significant discount on a subscription for our on-campus sports centre ! - Sustainable, inclusive and diverse work environment with an open culture, where you can be yourself and we pay attention to each other and to the world around us. Make the most of our bicycle budget, or join networks such as Young@EUR , FAME , EUR Disability Staff Network or QuEUR. - Time and space for your development in the broadest sense: development days and a personal career budget and free access to our university library . - Good pension with ABP that EUR contributes 2/3rds towards, discounts on various collective insurances with Zilveren Kruis Achmea, Loyalis, and Allianz, and compensation for travel, working from home and home internet use. - Are you currently combining your job with parenthood, or do you want to do so in the future? EUR offers partially paid parental leave and fully paid additional birth leave for partners , and our campus features a daycare (daycare not paid for by EUR). - Moving to the Netherlands for your job with EUR? Then you may be eligible for the Expat-ruling if you meet the requirements of the Belastingdienst (Dutch tax agency), and with our Dual Career Programme we will also help your partner find the right job for them. Employer Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) is an internationally oriented university with a strong social orientation in its education and research, as expressed in our mission ‘Creating positive societal impact’. EUR is home to 4.100 academics and professionals and almost 33.000 students from more than 140 countries. Everything we do, we do under the credo The Erasmian Way – Making Minds Matter. We’re global citizens, connecting, entrepreneurial, open-minded, and socially involved. These Erasmian Values function as our internal compass and create EUR’s distinctive and recognizable profile. From these values, with a broad perspective and with an eye for diversity, different backgrounds and opinions, our employees work closely together to solve societal challenges from the dynamic and cosmopolitan city of Rotterdam. Thanks to the high quality and positive societal impact of our research and education, EUR can compete with the top European universities. www.eur.nl . Faculty / Institute / Central service Rotterdam School of Management, Erasmus University (RSM) is one of Europe’s top-ranked business schools. RSM provides ground-breaking research and education furthering excellence in all aspects of management and is based in the international port city of Rotterdam – a vital nexus of business, logistics and trade. RSM’s primary focus is on developing business leaders with international careers who can become a force for positive change by carrying their innovative mindset into a sustainable future. Our first-class range of bachelor, master, MBA, PhD and executive programmes encourage them to become critical, creative, caring and collaborative thinkers and doers. RSM is an equal opportunity employer and explicitly encourages applications from candidates of all genders, ethnicities, and nationalities. www.rsm.nl Applying Interested in applying for this vacancy? We encourage you to submit your most recent CV and a motivation letter that outlines your interest in the role and your relevant experience. Please note that interviews may be scheduled during the recruitment period, therefore early applications are encouraged. The closing date for this vacancy is 13 November 2026. For more information about the role, please contact the Dean's Office facilities and services manager by email at avalk@rsm.nl . Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for Interim Secretary RSM Department of the Dean Close Interested? Interested? Compare 0 study programme Close Compare 0 study programme Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies Interim Secretary RSM Department of the Dean Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/phd-position-decision-neuroscience-0": {
      "title": "PhD position in Decision Neuroscience",
      "description": "PhD position in Decision Neuroscience | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies PhD position in Decision Neuroscience Listen Published Monday 2 Nov 2026 Deadline Friday 15 Jan 2027 Work area PhD Organisational unit Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) Salary € 3.204 - € 4.051 Employment 1 fte - 1 fte Abstract The Erasmus Center for Neuroeconomics seeks a highly motivated PhD student looking to study decision-making using neuroscience methods (fMRI or EEG). Strong applicants typically have backgrounds in (cognitive) psychology, (cognitive) neuroscience, neuroeconomics, or data science, and are looking to pursue careers as world-class academic researchers. At our Center, students define and execute their own projects in consultation with their advisers, and thus need creativity, self-direction, and a passion for scientific research. Keywords Neuroeconomics, consumer neuroscience, decision-making, fMRI, EEG Topic he Erasmus Center for Neuroeconomics is hosted by the Marketing department at the Rotterdam School of Management (RSM), which ranks among the best in the world. Department members publish their research in top journals in marketing as well as related fields such as psychology and neuroscience. They deeply care about open science practices (e.g., data sharing and open-source software), and frequently host seminars to encourage knowledge exchange. The group is diverse (in terms of research interests and cultural background), collaborative, and collegial. Our PhD program seeks to train the next generation of academics. We want our students to maximize their potential and become independent scholars. We expect students to become experts in and define a research agenda around a topic of their choosing. As such, PhD positions in our group are open. During their years of study, students define and execute their own projects. They do this in consultation with their advisers, but may also work with other faculty, including those at other institutes. Within the department, researchers at the Center for Neuroeconomics work on a wide range of topics in decision neuroscience such as understanding persuasive communication, emotions and their impact on choice, dishonesty, charitable giving, consumer judgments and predicting population-level outcomes from neural data. Projects of current PhDs in our Center focus on the role of story-telling and emotions in the persuasive appeal of advertising, how (false) information impacts on consumer judgment and decision-making, the persuasiveness of donation appeals, and the fundamental processes underlying neuroforecasting. In addition to standard required course work, decision neuroscience students typically take courses in neuroscience, experimental design, advanced statistics such as multi-level models, programming, machine learning, and seminars in consumer behavior. Moreover, the department offers a workshop series that includes seminars on a variety of topics, content, skills, and method related. Examples are: How to publish successfully, how to prepare for the job market, how to manage your data and code. These workshops are hosted by internal and external faculty members, which enables students to get to know excellent researchers and establish their own (international) network. Note: Applicants seeking to work at the intersection of neuroscience and another discipline (quantitative marketing and/or consumer behavior) should indicate this preference in their application and discuss their motivation for interdisciplinary research. Approach Experience with fMRI data acquisition and/or analysis is required for this position. Experience or interest in EEG data and more advanced fMRI analysis methods, such as multi-voxel pattern analysis (MVPA), representational similarity analysis (RSA), classification algorithms, prediction models, or multivariate approaches to fMRI and EEG data analyses is a definite plus. Experience with and affinity for programming, particularly in Python (or otherwise Matlab or R) is a must. In addition to fMRI and EEG, we make use of eye-tracking, facial coding, hormone measurement/administration and behavioral measures in our research. Students have access to world-class research facilities : Researchers have access to multiple MRI scanners at the Erasmus Medical Centre. The Erasmus Behavioral Lab provides facilities to conduct high-quality behavioral research, including sound-insulated cubicles, group labs, VR lab, video labs, and facilities for eye tracking, EEG/ERP, facial coding, and hormone-administration studies. High-performance computing is available to researchers via SURFSara (a Dutch consortium for scientific computing). Required profile We seek candidates with the following qualities: Intellectual curiosity, eagerness to learn, and openness to criticism and other perspectives Strong commitment to methodological rigor and scientific integrity Strong motivation to pursue an international career as a leading scholar Excellent speaking and writing ability in fluent English, ideally with experience writing for a scientific audience Willingness and motivation to independently formulate research projects and carry them through to completion Excellent organizational skills Masters’ degree (preferably a Research Master´s or MPhil degree) Background in cognitive neuroscience, cognitive psychology, neuroeconomics, or similar A definite interest in applying knowledge of the brain to societal (business) practice Prior experience analysing fMRI data using techniques such as MVPA, as well as neural reliability (inter-subject correlations in neural activity), or prior experience with applying multivariate techniques with EEG are a definite plus Required by ERIM All application documents required by ERIM can be found here . Expected output You will generate research that can be published in top-tier peer-reviewed journals in neuroscience (Journal of Neuroscience, Neuron, Social, Cognitive and Affective Neuroscience, Neuroimage), and marketing (Journal of Marketing Research, Journal of Consumer Research, Marketing Science, Journal of Marketing). The marketing group at RSM has a strong record publishing in these and other top journals in related fields, including Management, Psychology, and Economics. The final results of the PhD project are published in a PhD dissertation, and most chapters in marketing PhD dissertations at RSM find their way into top journals. Cooperation To strengthen your international research network and complement the time at RSM, students may receive funding for a 3- to 6-month research visit at a top international university. Past visits of PhD students from our department have included Stanford, Wharton, Harvard, Chicago, Colorado, Cornell, Columbia, and UCLA. At our Center we are well connected to international groups in consumer neuroscience and neuroeconomics. Societal relevance Students are encouraged to pursue topics that not only expand our knowledge of the brain, but also improve the practice of marketing and importantly, also improve consumer or societal well-being, aligning closely with the school’s mission to be a force for positive change in the world. Scientific relevance PhD research should be of the highest quality, carried out with scientific rigor and the utmost integrity. The department values openness and encourages students to embrace the principles and tools of open science (e.g., making code and data available to others and pre-registering experiments). The marketing group conducts research in our core field of marketing, as well as related disciplines such as management, psychology, judgment and decision making, neuroscience, economics, and statistics. Our diversity and interdisciplinarity make the department a lively, creative, and intellectually stimulating place to conduct research. Literature references & data sources Please visit the website for the Center for Neuroeconomics for more information on current research projects and to check out the profiles of the researchers affiliated with the Center. Recent publications: Genevsky, A. & Boksem, M.A.S. (2026) “Applications in Consumer Neuroscience: Decoding and Neuroforecasting” in Neuroeconomics: Core Topics and Current Directions, D. Smith, P. Lockwood, D. Fareri, Eds. (Springer Nature). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-02925-6_34 Boksem, M.A.S., van Diepen, R.M., Eijlers, E., Boekel, W., & Smidts, A. (2025). Do EEG metrics derived from trailers predict the commercial success of movies? A systematic analysis of five independent datasets. Journal of Marketing Research, 62(4), 703-720. https://doi.org/10.1177/00222437241309875 Genevsky, A., Yoon, C., Lin, T., Shaw, S., Knutson, B. (2025) The impact of affective congruence on charitable giving. Social Cognitive and Affective Neuroscience, 20(1). https://doi.org/10.1093/scan/nsaf086 Genevsky, A., Tong, L., Knutson, B., (2025) Neuroforecasting reveals generalizable components of choice. PNAS Nexus, 4(2). https://doi.org/10.1093/pnasnexus/pgaf029 Chan, H.-Y., Boksem, M.A.S., Venkratraman, V., Dietvorst, RC., Scholz, C., Vo, K., Falk, E. B., & Smidts, A. (2024). Neural signals of video advertisement liking: Insights into psychological processes and their temporal dynamics. Journal of Marketing Research, 61(5), 891-913. https://doi.org/10.1177/00222437231194319 Genevsky, A., (2025) Social credit scores reduce interpersonal cooperation and trust. PLoS One, 20(11). https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0335810 Van Brussel, L., Boksem, M.A.S., Dietvorst, R.C., & Smidts, A. (2024). Brain activity of professional investors signals future stock performance. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America, 121(16), e2307982121. Article e2307982121. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2307982121 Van Diepen, R.M., Boksem, M.A.S., Smidts A. (2024) Reliability of EEG metrics for assessing video advertisements. Journal of Advertising, 54(4), 506–526. https://doi.org/10.1080/00913367.2024.2418109 Speer, S.P.H., Keysers, C., Campdepadros Barrios, J. Teurlings, C.J.S., Smidts A., Boksem, M.A.S., Wager, T.D., Gazzola, V. (2023) A Multivariate Brain Signature for Reward. Neuroimage, 271, 119990. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2023.119990 Speer, S.P.H., Martinovici, A., Smidts, A., Boksem, M.A.S. (2023) The acute effects of stress on dishonesty are moderated by individual differences in moral default. Scientific Reports 13, 3984. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-31056-2 Speer, S. P. H., Smidts, A., & Boksem, M. A. S. (2022). Cognitive control and dishonesty. Trends in Cognitive Sciences, 26(9), 796 - 808. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tics.2022.06.005 Employment conditions ERIM offers fully-funded and salaried PhD positions, which means that accepted PhD candidates become employees (promovendi) of Erasmus University Rotterdam. Salary and benefits are in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO). Erasmus University Rotterdam aspires to be an equitable and inclusive community. We nurture an open culture, where everyone is supported to fulfil their full potential. We see inclusivity of talent as the basis of our successes, and the diversity of perspectives and people as a highly valued outcome. EUR provides equal opportunities to all employees and applicants regardless of gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, religion, ethnicity, age, neurodiversity, functional impairment, citizenship, or any other aspect which makes them unique. We look forward to welcoming you to our community. Contact information For questions regarding the PhD application and selection procedure, please check the Admissions or contact the PhD Doctoral Office via phdadmissions@erim.eur.nl . If you have any questions about the project, please contact Dr. Mirjam Tuk ( tuk@rsm.nl ). Note: In the Fall, we offer our PhD Virtual Open House Day. If you are interested in our PhD program and consider applying, we invite you to join us on November 30 from 9.30 am to 11.00 am CET OR on December 1 from 8.00 pm to 9.30 pm CET . In these info sessions we provide you with lots of information on our PhD program, department, and school. You will also get to know some of our faculty members and current PhD students, hear them talk about their research, and have the chance to ask questions. Interested? Then please register here or use this QR code to register: [Registration deadline: November 23] We look forward to meeting you! Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for PhD position in Decision Neuroscience Close Interested? Interested? Compare 0 study programme Close Compare 0 study programme Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies PhD position in Decision Neuroscience Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/phd-quantitative-marketing-0": {
      "title": "PhD in Quantitative Marketing",
      "description": "PhD in Quantitative Marketing | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies PhD in Quantitative Marketing Listen Published Monday 2 Nov 2026 Deadline Friday 15 Jan 2027 Work area PhD Organisational unit Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) Salary € 3.204 - € 4.051 Employment 1 fte - 1 fte Abstract The marketing group at Rotterdam School of Management, Erasmus University seeks highly motivated PhD students looking to study topics in the domain of quantitative marketing. Our group is unique in the Netherlands with both scholars focusing on developing and applying state-of-the-art methodologies from the fields of machine learning/AI, statistics, and economics, as well as scholars focusing on consumer behavior research that is deeply embedded in both theory and practice. Our faculty combines our methodological expertise with a deep understanding of the challenges businesses face. As part of a business school, we have strong ties with industry (profit and nonprofit) and government that allow our research to have direct societal impact. Strong applicants typically have backgrounds in computer science, statistics or econometrics. They are typically looking to pursue careers as world-class academic researchers. Students define and execute their own projects in consultation with their advisers and thus need creativity, self-direction, and a passion for scientific research. We are looking for candidates that are equally interested in high-quality academic research and in addressing real-world problems. Keywords Privacy, Digital Marketing, Machine Learning, Causal Inference Topic The marketing group at Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) ranks among the best in the world. Our members publish their research in top journals in marketing as well as related fields. They deeply care about open science practices (e.g., data sharing and open-source software) and frequently host seminars to encourage knowledge exchange. In addition, we have a track record of excellent academic placements. Around 80% of our graduated PhD’s started their first job in academia, and over 50% landed a job at a top 100 business school (UT Dallas ranking).  The group is diverse (in terms of research interests and cultural background), collaborative, and collegial. Our PhD program seeks to train the next generation of marketing academics. We want our students to maximize their potential and become independent marketing scholars. We expect students to become experts in their domain of interest and define a research agenda around a topic of their choosing. As such, PhD positions in our group are open and students have the opportunity to collaborate with several faculty members when possible. The supervisory team will be formed considering the student’s research interests. During their years of study, students define and execute their own projects. They do this in consultation with their advisers. Our quantitatively oriented faculty work on topics such as design of multi-armed bandits and reinforcement learning models with applications to recommendation systems and clinical trials (Gui Liberali), privacy (Gilian Ponte), causal inference (Jason Roos), consumer eye tracking (Ana Martinovici), digital marketing and behavioral decision making (Alina Ferecatu), virtual / augmented / mixed reality (Yvonne van Everdingen), digital platform markets (David Kusterer), marketing strategy (Gerrit van Bruggen), deep learning (Sebastian Gabel), causal inference and statistical learning (Xi Chen), customer analytics (Aurélie Lemmens), and consumer learning (Maciej Szymanowski). PhD students in our department receive excellent training and ample opportunities for feedback. In addition to standard required course work, students typically take courses in machine learning, (micro)economics, statistics, causal inference, econometrics, and seminars in quantitative marketing, both internally and externally. In addition, the department regularly offers workshops on a variety of topics, hosted by internal and external faculty members, which enables students to get to know excellent researchers and establish their own (international) network. To facilitate the latter, students are also encouraged and receive funding to go on a research visit to leading research-intensive international institutions and collaborate with excellent external faculty. Every year, we host a PhD day during which the students present their work and receive feedback from the department at large. Besides, we organize weekly lunchclubs during which both students and faculty can present early-stage work and receive feedback in a safe environment. For this vacancy, we explicitly invite candidates who are interested in one of the following topics to apply: The development and application of AI methods for adaptive decision-making, experimentation, and personalization, including multi-armed bandits, reinforcement learning, and agentic AI. This includes settings in which consumers or firms learn from experience, or that AI systems learn from ongoing interactions and autonomously adapt their decisions under uncertainty and privacy constraints. platform economics, especially feedback and rating systems, their design, biases, and informativeness, and misinformation such as fake reviews. Candidates who have a keen interest in one of these topics are encouraged to explicitly indicate this in their application. Approach The PhD student will work in close collaboration with the supervisory team and other faculty on tasks that include: Identifying novel research questions based on real-world phenomena. Understanding the theoretical foundations and state-of-the-art models in marketing science, economics, and computer science literatures relevant to understanding the phenomena. Identifying the fundamental variables, trade-offs and relationships that are most important to studying the phenomena and formalizing them in a measurement model. Developing and coding the appropriate algorithms and methods that implement the novel concepts and model. Gathering experimental or observational data to test hypotheses or highlight the strength and boundaries of the proposed methods. Identifying the critical assumptions needed to draw inferences from empirical results. Writing computer code to analyse experimental or secondary data according the best practices and tools in the relative sub-domain, including versioning (e.g., GitHub), pre-registration, data sharing and open science (e.g., Figshare). Presenting research findings at international conferences, in our PhD day and brownbag seminars. Writing up findings for publication in international journals. Attending classes and seminars (including those offered at other universities) to further develop thinking and research skills. Participating in and contributing to departmental research functions (PhD Day, research seminars, weekly research meetings). Teaching students (to a limited degree). Through workshops, research seminars, applied and theoretical research with faculty, and seminars on key disciplines that provide the foundations of the marketing discipline (statistics, economics, psychology), the PhD student will gain the requisite experience for independent work. The actual project will be defined by the student and the supervisory group and, thus, requires creativity, self-direction, and passion for top-notch scientific research. Students have access to world-class research facilities : Erasmus Behavioral Lab provides facilities to conduct high-quality behavioral research, including sound-insulated cubicles, group labs, video labs, and facilities for eye tracking, EEG/ERP, facial coding, and hormone-administration studies. High-performance computing is available to researchers via SURFSara (a Dutch consortium for scientific computing). Excellent research funding. Required profile We seek candidates with the following qualities: Intellectual curiosity, drive, eagerness to learn, and openness to criticism and other perspectives Strong motivation to pursue an international career as a leading scholar Strong commitment to methodological rigor and scientific integrity Strong passion for real-world problems faced by different types of organizations Excellent speaking and writing ability in fluent English, ideally with experience writing for a scientific audience Willingness and motivation to independently formulate research projects and carry them through to completion Excellent organizational skills Strong motivation to pursue an international career as a leading scholar Masters’ degree (preferably a Research Master´s or MPhil degree) in one of the following fields: computer science, data science, operations research, economics, econometrics, statistics, management, or marketing. The ideal candidate should also have some experience in programming (e.g., R, Python, Julia, Scala, Java, or C++) and in data analysis, management, preparation, and visualization, ideally as part of at least one end-to-end data science project. Required by ERIM All application documents required by ERIM can be found here . Expected output You will generate research that can be published in top-tier peer-reviewed journals in marketing, such as the Journal of Marketing Research, Marketing Science, and Journal of Marketing. The research group at RSM has a strong record of publishing in these and other top journals in related fields, including Management, Psychology, Neuroscience, and Economics. The final results of the PhD project are published in a PhD dissertation, and most marketing PhD dissertations at RSM find their way into top journals. In addition, we strongly encourage our members to publish their codes on open-science platforms and create packages to generate more impact and adhere to the principle of Open Science. Cooperation To strengthen your international research network and complement your time at RSM, you may receive funding for a 3- to 6-month research visit. Past visits have included Stanford, Wharton, Chicago, Columbia, Harvard, Colorado, Cornell, and UCLA. We also strongly encourage collaboration with our business partners who are active in multiple domains (digital, retail, consumer goods, nonprofits, B2B, fintech, etc.). Societal relevance Students are encouraged to pursue topics that not only improve the practice of marketing, but also consumer or societal well-being, and thus align closely with the school’s mission to be a force for positive change in the world. Scientific relevance PhD research should be of the highest quality, carried out with scientific rigor and the utmost integrity. The department values openness and encourages students to embrace the principles and tools of open science (e.g., making code and data available to others and pre-registering experiments). The marketing group conducts research in our core field of marketing, as well as related disciplines such as management, psychology, judgment and decision-making, neuroscience, economics, and statistics. Our diversity and interdisciplinarity make the department a lively, creative, and intellectually stimulating place to conduct research. Literature references & data sources Please refer to the web pages of our faculty members ( https://www.rsm.nl/research/departments/marketing-management/faculty/ ) for more information about their current research interests. You may also refer to our RSM Discovery section (see here https://discovery.rsm.nl/researchers/ ). It offers a collection of articles and videos that our faculty (and those from other departments) have created about their research. You may also look at the work done by several expert practices at ECDA: Trial Design and Experimentation: https://ecda.eur.nl/expert-practices/trial-design-experimentation/ Virtual and Augmented Reality: https://ecda.eur.nl/expert-practices/virtual-augmented-reality/ Customer Analytics: https://ecda.eur.nl/expert-practices/customer-analytics/ Employment conditions ERIM offers fully-funded and salaried PhD positions, which means that accepted PhD candidates become employees (promovendi) of Erasmus University Rotterdam. Salary and benefits are in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO). Erasmus University Rotterdam aspires to be an equitable and inclusive community. We nurture an open culture, where everyone is supported to fulfil their full potential. We see inclusivity of talent as the basis of our successes, and the diversity of perspectives and people as a highly valued outcome. EUR provides equal opportunities to all employees and applicants regardless of gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, religion, ethnicity, age, neurodiversity, functional impairment, citizenship, or any other aspect which makes them unique. We look forward to welcoming you to our community. Contact information For questions regarding the PhD application and selection procedure, please check the Admissions or send us an e-mail via phdadmissions@erim.eur.nl . Note: In the Fall, we offer our PhD Virtual Open House Day. If you are interested in our PhD program and consider applying, we invite you to join us on November 30 from 9.30 am to 11.00 am CET OR on December 1 from 8.00 pm to 9.30 pm CET . In these info sessions we provide you with lots of information on our PhD program, department, and school. You will also get to know some of our faculty members and current PhD students, hear them talk about their research, and have the chance to ask questions. Interested? Then please register here or use this QR code to register: [Registration deadline: November 23] We look forward to meeting you! Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for PhD in Quantitative Marketing Close Interested? Interested? Compare 0 study programme Close Compare 0 study programme Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies PhD in Quantitative Marketing Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/research-accounting-and-taxation": {
      "title": "Research in accounting and taxation",
      "description": "Research in accounting and taxation | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Research in accounting and taxation Listen Published Monday 2 Nov 2026 Deadline Friday 15 Jan 2027 Work area PhD Organisational unit Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) Salary € 3.204 - € 4.051 Employment 1 fte - 1 fte Abstract In this Ph.D. project, you can work at the exciting intersection of taxation and managerial accounting. For example, you can study how changes in tax legislation or tax treatments affect executive compensation, performance targets, or the organizational structure of the firm. Taxes serve three main purposes: 1) Generate revenue for governments to fund their expenditures, 2) redistribute wealth within societies, and 3) steer behavior. Taxes are of great economic significance to companies as they constitute a major cash outflow and affect the group structures of multinationals. Firms react to tax incentives in various ways, including tax avoidance and compliance as well as investment and locational decisions. In the large literature on accounting and taxation, two topics that have not received much attention in prior literature are (1) the role of taxation in compensation contract design, and (2) the effect of taxation on organizational design. Compensation contract design is a topic in the broader area of managerial accounting and corporate governance and refers to how boards structure the compensation packages of CEOs and other executive managers to ensure that their own personal interests align with the overall objectives of the organization and overall shareholder value creation. Organizational design broadly refers to how firms organize their economic activity along markets, product lines, and information channels. Previous research from an accounting perspective in this area has looked at how much decision authority is assigned to local subsidiaries vis-à-vis the corporate headquarters, how many hierarchical and management layers are deployed in a firm to govern economic activity, and how the firm adjusts it labor resources in response to changes in the external environment by hiring and releasing employees. Keywords Business taxation, managerial accounting, executive compensation, labor adjustment, organizational design Topic Corporate taxation affects firms’ after-tax profitability and cash flows, which has consequences for investment, financing, and organizational choices (Hanlon and Heitzman, 2010). Changes in tax rates or the tax treatment of specific business activities can also affect how firms evaluate managers and organize their workforce. For instance, a tax reduction can raise reported earnings without improving operating performance, while higher cash tax payments can leave less funds available for compensation, hiring, or expansion. This Ph.D. project examines how changes in corporate taxation affect compensation contract design and organizational design. The compensation angle focuses on the relation between taxation and the reliance on different compensation elements (salary, bonus, and equity), performance targets, performance metrics, pay–performance sensitivity, or the use of relative performance evaluation. The organizational perspective concerns decentralization, hierarchical layers, and labor adjustment through hiring and layoffs. The overall objective of this project is to increase our understanding of how firms adjust these arrangements when taxation changes their profitability, cash flows, or incentives to organize activities in particular ways. Taxation and compensation contract design Agency theory posits that when managerial effort and actions are unobservable, compensation contracts must balance incentive provision against the costs of exposing managers to risk (Holmström, 1979). Taxation affects this trade-off because after-tax performance reflects both managerial decisions and changes in tax rules. Boards therefore need to determine how tax-related changes in reported performance should be reflected in managerial rewards. Moreover, performance metrics that are correlated with effective tax planning may also reward gains that arise without managerial intervention. Tax savings may also create opportunities for managerial rent extraction. Lower tax payments increase the resources available within the firm, but governance and compensation design influence how these resources are used. Strong oversight may help ensure that tax savings support investment or benefit shareholders, whereas weak oversight may allow managers to capture part of these gains through higher compensation or projects that provide private benefits (Desai, Dyck, and Zingales, 2007; Desai and Dharmapala, 2006; Fan, Johnson, and Pfeiffer, 2023). Previous literature has studied some aspects related to this trade-off. For instance, Phillips (2003) links the use of after-tax performance measures for business-unit managers to lower effective tax rates. Andreani, Ellahie, and Shivakumar (2025) find that weakly monitored CEOs receive compensation for tax windfall gains without corresponding penalties for losses. These studies provide initial empirical evidence on how tax outcomes are reflected in managerial compensation. Another potential research question could be how boards incorporate tax changes into performance targets, metric selection, and incentive strength. For example, a tax-related increase in earnings could make an existing earnings target easier to achieve and thereby affect the incentive strength of the underlying contract. In response, boards may adjust targets, change the weight placed on after-tax earnings, or use measures that exclude tax effects. Relative performance evaluation, which assesses performance against peer firms, could help to filter out shared tax shocks. Another possibility would be to study how taxation affects bonus payments through the internal availability of cash. Core and Guay (1999) show that firms use equity grants to adjust CEOs’ equity incentives. Corporate tax savings could increase the availability of cash for bonus payments or reduce the need to conserve cash through equity compensation without eliminating the incentive-related reasons for granting equity. The project could therefore examine whether governance and compensation design shape how tax-related cash-flow gains are allocated among executive compensation, shareholder distributions, and investment. Taxation may also change the risks that firms want managers to take. Tax rates and loss-offset provisions affect the returns to risky projects, while equity-based compensation can encourage managers to pursue riskier investment and tax-planning strategies. Firms may therefore adjust executives’ risk-taking incentives following tax reforms to maintain the desired balance between encouraging valuable projects and limiting excessive risk. The project could examine whether changes in compensation influence investment, tax planning, and the allocation of organizational resources (Rego and Wilson, 2012; Ljungqvist, Zhang, and Zuo, 2017; Langenmayr and Lester, 2018). Taxation and organizational design Organizational economics examines how firms divide decision authority and coordinate economic activity (Aghion and Tirole, 1997). Delegation allows local managers to use information that headquarters may not possess, but it does increase coordination costs and agency problems. For instance, Rajan and Wulf (2006) show that managerial layers and reporting relationships change with changes in incentives. Tax reforms may affect both the authority assigned to local managers and the number of management levels needed to oversee activities. Tax rules can also affect the benefits of coordinating decisions across business units. For example, a manager of a high-tax subsidiary may favor an internal transfer price that increases the subsidiary’s reported profit but also raises the group’s tax bill (Baldenius, Melumad, and Reichelstein, 2004). Taxation thus creates interdependencies between business units because a decision that benefits one unit may impose tax costs elsewhere in the organization. This increases the value of central coordination but may reduce the benefits of delegating decisions to local managers. Firms may respond by centralizing tax-related decisions, strengthening the role of the corporate tax function, or placing greater weight on group-level rather than business-unit performance measures. More complex tax rules may also increase the need for managers and specialists who coordinate decisions across units (Cools, Emmanuel, and Jorissen, 2008). Changes in taxation may consequently affect the relative benefits of centralized control and local discretion, as well as the need for managers who coordinate activities across units. Compensation and organizational design may also adjust jointly. When tax-related decision rights are moved between local managers, headquarters, and tax specialists, the responsibility for tax outcomes changes as well. Firms may consequently need to revise performance measures and incentive contracts to ensure that managers are evaluated on outcomes they can influence. For instance, Gallemore, Labro, and Scanlon (2025) associate tax-induced organizational complexity with longer performance horizons and adjusted performance measures. Building on this literature, the project could study how tax-related changes in responsibilities and organizational structure are associated with changes in managerial performance evaluation and incentive provision. Finally, there may be consequences of taxation for labor adjustment. When hiring employees, firms incur costs in recruiting and training employees and may lose valuable skills when they reduce employment. The literature on cost-stickiness shows that managers often choose to retain committed resources during economic downturns rather than reduce them immediately (Anderson, Banker, and Janakiraman, 2003). Corporate tax changes can affect these decisions by changing the cash available to maintain employment and the after-tax returns to expanding operations. For instance, Giroud and Rauh (2019) document that firms relocate establishments across jurisdictions in response to state taxation. The project could further examine whether tax reforms change firms’ demand for tax specialists and coordinating managers or alter the location of tax-related decision-making within the organization (see, e.g., Barrios and Gallemore, 2024). Research approach and contribution The project will use tax reforms and other changes in corporate tax treatment, combined with archival data. ExecuComp and Incentive Lab provide information on executive compensation and compensation contract elements, Revelio Labs has workforce histories, employee movements, occupations, and seniority classifications, and Compustat has other financial information required to construct adequate controls. Approach The research methodology used in this project is archival-based, using financial (accounting and tax) data from commercial databases and panel data econometric methods. Required profile The candidate has (a) a background in taxation, accounting, finance, economics, or econometrics, (b) good quantitative skills, and (c) an international focus. Required by ERIM All application documents required by ERIM can be found here . Cooperation The decision to cooperate with other universities or research groups depends on future project choices. It is expected, however, that the Ph.D. candidate will spend at least 6 months at an international university as a visiting scholar. Societal relevance Taxation serves several purposes in societies, such as 1) generating revenue for governments to fund their expenditures, 2) redistributing wealth within societies, and 3) steering behavior. This wide-ranging set of consequences can affect the way publicly listed firms remunerate their executives and organize their economic activity. Given the recent discussions on excessive compensation of large public firms and how labor markets absorb new talent, the project can provide important insights into existing societal challenges. Scientific relevance Tax legislation and tax research are quickly developing areas where many new laws are passed to curb tax avoidance and enhance transparency and tax enforcement. At the same time, there is relatively little interdisciplinary research that looks at the implications of taxation for areas such as corporate governance and organizational design, which is an opportunity to provide new perspectives and insights by investigating questions at the intersection of these fields. Literature references & data sources Aghion, P., and J. Tirole. 1997. Formal and Real Authority in Organizations. Journal of Political Economy 105 (1): 1–29. https://doi.org/10.1086/262063 Anderson, M. C., R. D. Banker, and S. N. Janakiraman. 2003. Are Selling, General, and Administrative Costs “Sticky”? Journal of Accounting Research 41 (1): 47–63. https://doi.org/10.1111/1475-679X.00095 Andreani, M., A. Ellahie, and L. Shivakumar. 2025. Are CEOs Rewarded for Luck? Evidence from Corporate Tax Windfalls. The Journal of Finance 80 (4): 2255–2302. https://doi.org/10.1111/jofi.13448 Baldenius, T., N. D. Melumad, and S. Reichelstein. 2004. Integrating Managerial and Tax Objectives in Transfer Pricing. The Accounting Review 79 (3): 591–615. https://doi.org/10.2308/accr.2004.79.3.591 Barrios, J. M., and J. Gallemore. 2024. Tax Planning Knowledge Diffusion via the Labor Market. Management Science 70 (2): 1194–1215. https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/mnsc.2023.4741 Cools, Martine, Clive Emmanuel, and Ann Jorissen. 2008. “Management Control in the Transfer Pricing Tax Compliant Multinational Enterprise.” Accounting, Organizations and Society 33 (6): 603–628. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aos.2007.05.004 . Core, J., and W. Guay. 1999. The Use of Equity Grants to Manage Optimal Equity Incentive Levels. Journal of Accounting and Economics 28 (2): 151–184. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0165-4101(99)00019-1 Desai, M. A., A. Dyck, and L. Zingales. 2007. “Theft and Taxes.” Journal of Financial Economics 84 (3): 591–623. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfineco.2006.05.005 Desai, M. A., and D. Dharmapala. 2006. “Corporate Tax Avoidance and High-Powered Incentives.” Journal of Financial Economics 79 (1): 145–179. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfineco.2005.02.002 Fan, Q., N. B. Johnson, and T. Pfeiffer. 2023. “The Impact of Intrafirm Incentive Conflicts on the Interplay Between Tax Incidence and Economic Efficiency.” Contemporary Accounting Research 40 (4): 2173–2202. https://doi.org/10.1111/1911-3846.12871 Gallemore, J., E. Labro, and G. Scanlon. 2025. Tax-Induced Organizational Complexity and Executive Performance Measurement. Working Paper. https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4606256 Giroud, X., and J. Rauh. 2019. State Taxation and the Reallocation of Business Activity: Evidence from Establishment-Level Data. Journal of Political Economy 127 (3): 1262–1316. https://doi.org/10.1086/701357 Hanlon, M., and S. Heitzman. 2010. A Review of Tax Research. Journal of Accounting and Economics 50 (2–3): 127–178. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacceco.2010.09.002 Holmström, B. 1979. Moral Hazard and Observability. The Bell Journal of Economics 10 (1): 74–91. https://doi.org/10.2307/3003320 Langenmayr, D., and R. Lester. 2018. “Taxation and Corporate Risk-Taking.” The Accounting Review 93 (3): 237–266. https://doi.org/10.2308/accr-51872 Ljungqvist, A., L. Zhang, and L. Zuo. 2017. “Sharing Risk with the Government: How Taxes Affect Corporate Risk Taking.” Journal of Accounting Research 55 (3): 669–707. https://doi.org/10.1111/1475-679X.12157 Phillips, J. D. 2003. Corporate Tax-Planning Effectiveness: The Role of Compensation-Based Incentives. The Accounting Review 78 (3): 847–874. https://doi.org/10.2308/accr.2003.78.3.847 Rajan, R. G., and J. Wulf. 2006. The Flattening Firm: Evidence from Panel Data on the Changing Nature of Corporate Hierarchies. The Review of Economics and Statistics 88 (4): 759–773. https://doi.org/10.1162/rest.88.4.759 Rego, S. O., and R. Wilson. 2012. “Equity Risk Incentives and Corporate Tax Aggressiveness.” Journal of Accounting Research 50 (3): 775–810. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1475-679X.2012.00438.x Employment conditions ERIM offers fully-funded and salaried PhD positions, which means that accepted PhD candidates become employees (promovendi) of Erasmus University Rotterdam. Salary and benefits are in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO). Erasmus University Rotterdam aspires to be an equitable and inclusive community. We nurture an open culture, where everyone is supported to fulfil their full potential. We see inclusivity of talent as the basis of our successes, and the diversity of perspectives and people as a highly valued outcome. EUR provides equal opportunities to all employees and applicants regardless of gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, religion, ethnicity, age, neurodiversity, functional impairment, citizenship, or any other aspect which makes them unique. We look forward to welcoming you to our community. Contact information For questions regarding the PhD application and selection procedure, please check the Admissions or contact the PhD Doctoral Office at phdadmissions@erim.eur.nl . Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for Research in accounting and taxation Close Interested? Interested? Compare 0 study programme Close Compare 0 study programme Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies Research in accounting and taxation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/optimization-emergency-services-under-decentralized-capacity-sources": {
      "title": "Optimization of emergency services under decentralized capacity sources",
      "description": "Optimization of emergency services under decentralized capacity sources | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Optimization of emergency services under decentralized capacity sources Listen Published Monday 2 Nov 2026 Deadline Friday 15 Jan 2027 Work area PhD Organisational unit Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) Salary € 3.204 - € 4.051 Employment 1 fte - 1 fte Abstract Emergency response systems are critical for saving lives, yet many struggle to maintain quick and reliable service. In High-Income Countries (HICs), rising demand and staff shortages put pressure on response times, while in Low- and Middle-Income Countries (LMICs), the lack of centralized coordination leads to significant delays. This research addresses these global challenges by exploring innovative capacity sources, such as the use of lay-person volunteers as community first responders (CFRs) and consolidation of independent ambulance providers through an emergency platform. These capacity sources operate independently from the emergency service and their location and availability is therefore unpredictable and uncontrollable. This project investigates how to integrate and manage decentralized, unpredictable capacity sources. The existing methodology in literature on emergency systems cannot capture this level of capacity-side uncertainty and new models need to be developed. The project will address challenges related to the estimation of performance, providing incentives for commitment, optimization of dispatch decisions, and resource allocation. The research will use mathematical modelling, including Integer Linear Programming, Markov Decision Processes, and Game Theory, to quantify performance and optimize operational processes in the presence of uncertain capacity. Partnerships with emergency providers in Kenya, Indonesia, Sweden and the Netherlands will ensure the embedding of theoretical advancements in real-world settings. By combining academic innovation with practical relevance, this project creates a solutions for addressing pressing challenges in emergency care. Faculty to contact: Dr. Pieter van den Berg Keywords Operations Management, Emergency Response, Public Sector, Platform Economy, Community First Responders. Topic Setting Emergency response systems increasingly rely on independent, unpredictable capacity sources such as commercial ambulances operating on emergency platforms and Community First Responders (CFRs) who volunteer through smartphone applications. These responders are not scheduled, positioned, or controlled by the emergency service. Their location, availability, commitment, and capabilities fluctuate, creating a fundamentally different type of uncertainty than the demand-side uncertainty traditionally studied in emergency service research. This shift creates major operational challenges. Emergency platforms in LMICs depend on commercial ambulances that strategically choose when and where to be available, driven by revenue considerations rather than system-wide performance. CFR systems in HICs rely on volunteers who decide whether to accept alerts based on personal convenience and perceived value, resulting in acceptance rates well below 50%. In both cases, emergency services cannot use demand-side pricing, cannot force responders to relocate, and therefore cannot guarantee coverage in low-demand areas. Academic research has only begun to explore these issues. Work on CFRs focuses mainly on dispatching for cardiac arrest, while research on emergency platforms has only just started to appear. The ride-hailing literature offers useful analogies, but its core mechanisms—surge pricing, access limitations, selective service areas—are ethically unacceptable in emergency care. This leaves emergency services without the analytical tools needed to integrate decentralized capacity sources effectively. The PhD project will address this gap by developing new operational models that capture how decentralized responders behave and how emergency services can influence and coordinate them. The project will combine game theory, information design, machine learning, and optimization, using real-world data from partners in Kenya, Indonesia, Sweden, and the Netherlands. Research Problem Emergency services lack the analytical and operational frameworks needed to integrate independent, unpredictable responders whose behavior cannot be directly controlled. This limits their ability to estimate performance, influence responder decisions, and dispatch heterogeneous responders effectively. Central Research Question How can emergency services most effectively integrate and manage independent, unpredictable capacity sources to improve response times and reliability? Approach Mathematical Modelling, Machine Learning, Simulation, Optimization, Game Theory. Required profile We seek a candidate who has: A master in operations research, operations management, business analytics, mathematics, industrial engineering, computer science or similar Programming skills Good communication skills in English Required by ERIM All application documents required by ERIM can be found here . Applications will be evaluated as they come in. Expected output We expect you to, together with the supervisory team, conduct research that is publishable in top academic journals. See https://www.erim.eur.nl/about-erim/erim-journals-list-ejl/ for a list of Primary Star (P*), and Primary (P) journals. Cooperation This PhD position is part of the Vidi project awarded to dr. Pieter L. van den Berg. Besides this PhD project, the project will also fund a PostDoc position. During the PhD project, you are expected to work together with the PostDoc as well as the industry partners. Societal relevance To ensure the societal relevance of the project, you are expected to collaborate with industry partners in the emergency response sector. The envisioned supervisory team has a strong record on having practical impact with high quality research. Scientific relevance The Supply Chain Management group at RSM has a strong track record of publishing research in highly ranked management journals. In addition to undertaking research visits, PhD students are actively encouraged to participate in all major international conferences, and are supported in their travel to disseminate their research findings. Literature references & data sources See the following two papers for an impression of the type of research conducted in this project. P.L. van den Berg, S.G. Henderson, C.J. Jagtenberg, M. Li. Modeling the Impact of Community First Responders . Management Science 71(2): 992-1008, 2025. P.L. van den Berg, A. Calmon, A. Gernert, S. Lemmens, M. Rabinovich, G. Romero. The Value of Time- and Location-Commitment for Decentralized Emergency Medical Services . To appear in Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, 2026. Employment conditions ERIM offers fully-funded and salaried PhD positions, which means that accepted PhD candidates become employees (promovendi) of Erasmus University Rotterdam. Salary and benefits are in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO). Erasmus University Rotterdam aspires to be an equitable and inclusive community. We nurture an open culture, where everyone is supported to fulfil their full potential. We see inclusivity of talent as the basis of our successes, and the diversity of perspectives and people as a highly valued outcome. EUR provides equal opportunities to all employees and applicants regardless of gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, religion, ethnicity, age, neurodiversity, functional impairment, citizenship, or any other aspect which makes them unique. We look forward to welcoming you to our community. Contact information For questions regarding the PhD application and selection procedure, please check the Admissions or contact the PhD Doctoral Office at phdadmissions@erim.eur.nl . Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for Optimization of emergency services under decentralized capacity sources Close Interested? Interested? Compare 0 study programme Close Compare 0 study programme Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies Optimization of emergency services under decentralized capacity sources Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/two-year-postdoc-department-people-organisations": {
      "title": "Two-year Postdoc, Department People & Organisations",
      "description": "Two-year Postdoc, Department People & Organisations | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies Two-year Postdoc, Department People & Organisations Listen Published Monday 2 Nov 2026 Deadline Monday 30 Nov 2026 Work area Management Organisational unit Rotterdam School of Management (RSM) Salary € 5.932 - € 7.573 Employment 0.8 fte - 0.8 fte Introduction This two-year postdoc is an exciting opportunity for an early career researcher to continue to develop their research skills and grow their research pipeline in an interesting and rich field context. You will work closely with Dr Rebecca (Bex) Hewett on a longitudinal study in partnership with the Social Innovation Academy ( SINA ), a network of academies supporting disadvantaged young people to become social entrepreneurs. The fieldwork in this project will take place in one or more SINA academy; based across multiple African countries (originating in Uganda), Nepal, and soon Germany. This position offers you an intellectually open environment, strong methodological support and the freedom to develop an independent research trajectory while contributing to a coherent, high‑impact project. If you enjoy working with rich data, care deeply about theory, and want your work to have both scholarly and societal impact, this role is for you. As a postdoctoral fellow you will be part of the department of People and Organisations ( www.rsm.nl/faculty-research/departments/people-and-organisations/ ) at Rotterdam School of Management, Erasmus University (RSM) ( www.rsm.nl/ ). We are an exceptionally supportive, research-focused, international group of faculty and PhDs. We take pride in our supportive, high-performance research culture. Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) ( www.eur.nl/en ) ranks third in Europe in terms of 2020-2024 UTD top journal output. RSM’s faculty members routinely publish in top-tier journals like the Academy of Management Journal, Academy of Management Review, Administrative Science Quarterly, Strategic Management Journal, Journal of Management Studies, Journal of Management, Organization Science and Management Science. Additionally, RSM faculty members regularly serve as editors and associate editors of top journals like Academy of Management Journal, Academy of Management Review, Journal of Management and Journal of Management Studies. We are also proud of our outstanding PhD programme, which has a strong record of placements at institutions like MIT, INSEAD, HEC, Cambridge University (Judge), University of Warwick, University of South Carolina (Moore), Aalto University, BI Norwegian, and McGill University. Job description SINA provides a rich empirical context to explore many potential questions, puzzles, and phenomena. Our research could draw on democratic organisation and participation, the experiences of young people, their learning processes, community dynamics (within SINA and more broadly), social entrepreneurship, the functioning of this network organisation as a whole, or social and institutional dynamics in the local context. Bex Hewett became interested in SINA via their strong focus on decentralised organising and the principle of “freesponsibility” (freedom and responsibility) that both guide the learning experiences of young people. Bex has a particular interest in exploring process and system dynamics. The focus of the project will be driven partly by the interests of the successful candidate, with SINA as active partners in the research. We will work together to co-create research questions, study design, and developing conclusions. Throughout the project you will be working closely with Bex and collaborators at RSM and elsewhere (TBD based on the decided focus of the project). The research culture and resources set by RSM’s research institute, Erasmus Research Institute of Management (ERIM) ( www.erim.eur.nl/ ), aim to provide you with the best conditions in which to conduct outstanding research. The project aims to create scholarly impact (via conference presentations and high impact journal publications); practical impact via recommendations to SINA; and broader societal impact via knowledge dissemination activities. The ideal candidate has experience conducting organisational ethnography, or at least minimum ethnographic methods (including field observation and interviews). In addition to having the required strong social and communication skills essential for ethnographic work, you will bring your experience to actively contribute across the research process, including; data collection (e.g., observation, interviewing), data management (field-note taking, archival record management), analysis, theory development, and writing papers for leading journals. It would be beneficial if you have experience of fieldwork in the Global South, particularly in one of the countries in which SINA operates, but this is not essential. We encourage you to initiate new research questions, explore methodological innovations, and develop an independent research profile that complements the broader project. Job requirements You will bring your experience of qualitative methods to explore process and/or system dynamics across a range of potential phenomena in the context of SINA as a field site. For this position: You possess a doctorate degree in management, organisation studies, sociology, anthropology, political economy, or other related qualitative, social science domains. Your PhD research made use of ethnographic methods. You have completed your PhD, or you may be in your final year of dissertation research with a clear plan for completion. This is a part-time (80%) temporary position with a contract for 30.4 hours per week starting early 2027 (date to be mutually agreed). The contract duration will be for two years. There may be the possibility to extend the contract to 100% via a small teaching load, but this is not guaranteed. Employment conditions and benefits We offer you an internationally oriented and varied position in an enthusiastic team, with excellent employment conditions in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU). This position is 0.8 FTE, and the start date is early 2027. You will be based at Campus Woudestein in Rotterdam at Rotterdam School of Management, Erasmus University (RSM). The salary depends on your knowledge and experience, and for a full-time appointment (38 hours) would amount to between €4,925 and €6,682 gross per month (Scale 11), in accordance with the CAO-NU. The contract will be entered into for a period of one (1) year and, if proven suitable and continued in the same position, under unchanged (financial and/or organisational) circumstances, will be extended for a second year. Everything else we have to offer can be found below: Everything you need for a good work-life balance : working from home by arrangement, 41 holiday days based on a 40-hour working week, 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% year-end bonus, and a significant discount on a membership at our campus sports centre . A sustainable, inclusive and diverse working environment ( https://www.eur.nl/en/about-eur/vision/inclusion-diversity-equity-access ) with an open culture where you can be yourself and we look out for one another and for the world. Take advantage of our cycling allowance, for example, or participate in networks such as Young@EUR , Fame , or QuEUR. Time and space for your development in the broadest sense: development days and a personal career budget and free access to our university library . A good pension with ABP to which we contribute two-thirds, discounts on various collective insurance schemes with Zilveren Kruis Achmea, Loyalis, Allianz, and a commuting, working-from-home and internet allowance. Combining your job with parenthood, or is this something you're planning for the future? EUR offers partially paid parental leave and fully paid supplementary birth leave for partners, and there is a childcare facility on campus (childcare at your own expense). Moving to the Netherlands for a job at EUR? You may be able to make use of the Expat Scheme if you meet the requirements of the Dutch Tax Authority, and our Dual Career Programme also helps your partner find a great job. Employer Erasmus University Rotterdam (EUR) is an internationally oriented university with a distinct social vision on education and research – our mission being 'Creating positive societal impact'. EUR employs over 4,100 staff and nearly 33,000 students from 140 countries follow our academic education. Everything we do, we do under the credo The Erasmian Way - Making Minds Matter. We are global citizens: connecting, entrepreneurial, open-minded and socially engaged. These Erasmian Values serve as our internal compass and make Erasmus University recognisable and distinctive to the outside world. Guided by these values, with a broad perspective and an eye for diversity, different backgrounds and viewpoints, our staff collaborate closely on solving societal challenges from dynamic and cosmopolitan Rotterdam. Thanks in part to the high quality and positive societal impact of our education and research, and our characteristic mentality, EUR can measure itself against the top European universities. www.eur.nl Faculty / Institute / Central service Rotterdam School of Management, Erasmus University (RSM) is one of Europe’s top-ranked business schools. RSM provides ground-breaking research and education furthering excellence in all aspects of management and is based in the international port city of Rotterdam – a vital nexus of business, logistics and trade. RSM’s primary focus is on developing business leaders with international careers who can become a force for positive change by carrying their innovative mindset into a sustainable future. Our first-class range of bachelor, master, MBA, PhD and executive programmes encourage them to become critical, creative, caring and collaborative thinkers and doers. RSM is an equal opportunity employer and explicitly encourages applications from candidates of all genders, ethnicities, and nationalities. www.rsm.nl Department The Department of People and Organisations produces high-quality research and provides excellent teaching of the management of people in organisations. Our diverse faculty apply micro- and macro-perspectives to understand the dynamics of organisations and their employees, and organisations’ interaction with their environment. Applying Are you interested in this vacancy? Please submit your application documents before midnight (CET) on 30 November 2026 . Depending on your availability, interviews will be conducted virtually or in-person. We will continue to receive applications until the position has been filled. Please ensure that your application includes all of the following: CV Cover letter including your motivation for the position A job market paper Research statement, including a brief outline of research ideas connecting your experience to the SINA context Three letters of recommendation Do you have any questions or need more information about this vacancy? Please contact Bex Hewett via email at hewett@rsm.nl . Stay up to date with new vacancies! Want to be the first to know about vacancies that match your interests? Let us know what you're looking for, and we'll send you an email as soon as a new opportunity that fits your profile is posted.\nDon’t miss out on your perfect job! Sign up Close Stay up to date with new vacancies! Share this page Interested? Applicant portal Open navigation for Two-year Postdoc, Department People & Organisations Close Interested? Interested? Compare 0 study programme Close Compare 0 study programme Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies Two-year Postdoc, Department People & Organisations Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
    },
    "https://www.eur.nl/en/working-at-eur/vacancies/junior-jurist-rechtsbescherming-08-10-fte": {
      "title": "(Junior) Jurist Rechtsbescherming (0,8 – 1,0 fte)",
      "description": "(Junior) Jurist Rechtsbescherming (0,8 – 1,0 fte) | Working at | Erasmus University Rotterdam Skip to main content Skip to search Skip to subnavigation Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Close Open search Close Open menu Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Switch language to EN English current language NL Nederlands Search Close Search Search Primary Why work at EUR Open submenu Why work at EUR Campus Diversity Societal involvement Sustainability Working conditions Working areas Open submenu Working areas Academic staff Working in Finance Working in IT Working at Legal Affairs Working at Real Estate & Facilities PhD LDE traineeship International staff Practical information Open submenu Practical information Candidate portal Vacancies (Junior) Jurist Rechtsbescherming (0,8 – 1,0 fte) Listen This vacancy is only available in Dutch. Use the button below to view it in Dutch. View vacancy in Dutch Open navigation for (Junior) Jurist Rechtsbescherming (0,8 – 1,0 fte) Close Compare 0 study programme Close Compare 0 study programme Compare study programmes Breadcrumb Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Vacancies (Junior) Jurist Rechtsbescherming (0,8 – 1,0 fte) Working at Erasmus University Rotterdam Directly to Information Directly to Why work at EUR Working areas International staff Practical information Vacancies Information ANBI Cookies Disclaimer Privacy statement Accessibility Our channels Facebook Instagram X (formerly twitter) Linkedin Youtube Making Minds Matter Watch the video Close"
